139538705 17592186045443 true] [17592186672734 1074 "We're told that the temperature of the cooling iron is modeled by the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.03(T-30).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-30} & =-0.03\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-30} & =-\\int 0.03\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-30| & =-0.03t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-30>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-30 & ={e}^{-0.03t+C} \\\\ T-30 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-0.03t} \\\\ T-30 & =K{e}^{-0.03t} \\\\ T & =30+K{e}^{-0.03t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature of the iron was ${119}^{∘}C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=119,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}119 & =30+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 119 & =30+K \\\\ K & =89\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nT(t)=30+89{e}^{-0.03t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the temperature of the iron after $t=4$ minutes is\n\n$$\nT(4)=30+89{e}^{-0.03\\cdot 4}≈{109}^{∘}\\text{C},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer." 13194139538705 17592186045443 true] [17592186672735 1080 "{115}^{∘}\\text{C}" 13194139538705 17592186045443 true] [17592186672735 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139538705 17592186045443 true] [17592186672735 1083 #uuid "0be068eb-64c8-59a3-afa7-5664f17d1b0c" 13194139538705 17592186045443 true] [17592186672736 1075 #edb/ref 17592186672735 13194139538705 17592186045443 true] [17592186672736 1075 #edb/ref 17592186672738 13194139538705 17592186045443 true] [17592186672736 1075 #edb/ref 17592186672747 13194139538705 17592186045443 true] [17592186672736 1075 #edb/ref 17592186672751 13194139538705 17592186045443 true] [17592186672736 1075 #edb/ref 17592186672757 13194139538705 17592186045443 true] [17592186672736 1076 #edb/ref 17592186